{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good-cause requirement","requirement":"A residential landlord may take possession only by tenant consent or legal eviction, must have good cause to evict or not renew a written or oral lease, must describe the cause in a written notice to quit, and must obtain a Judgment for Possession.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; written and oral residential leases. Each ground except nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in the notice, and a Notice to Cease may also be required.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and unconscionability limit","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; the lease must be terminated and the tenant offered a new lease at the increased rent with proper written notice. The increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies in New Jersey; an increase must also comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A tenant may complain to a municipal rent control board; where there is none, the landlord may sue for nonpayment of the increase and a judge decides whether it is unconscionable.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"Rent increases must also comply with municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinances where one exists.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after construction of the building is completed; rents subsidized by government funding may also be exempt.","key_value":"30 years following completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the exemption runs for 30 years from completion of construction. Rents subsidized by government funding may also be exempt from local rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; 30-year period runs from completion of construction"},"exemptions":"Rents that are subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Exempts such buildings from municipal rent control ordinances.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion; increases for new services or amenities are permitted.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in the converted building, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not prohibited.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer and return duties on sale of the property","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing the deposit, giving notices, paying interest and returning the deposit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies; security deposit disputes are decided by the courts, and no State agency has jurisdiction over them.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Whether or not the deposit and interest are transferred, the new owner is responsible for the investment of the security deposit, giving all notices and paying interest, and for the return of the security deposit, plus any accrued earnings or interest (N.J.S.A. 46:8-21).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalty for willfully withholding deposits of tenants receiving public assistance","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant receiving State or federal financial assistance may be penalized, and the tenant need not sue to keep program eligibility.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who received financial assistance through any State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or more than $2,000 for each offense, collected by summary proceeding, payable to the State entity that made the payments; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance, may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source-of-income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in the rental of housing based on a person's source of lawful income, among other protected characteristics, and must treat applicants equally.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned for and occupied by one sex (as to familial status).","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Exemptions turn on owner-occupancy of two-family dwellings and single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex and age-restricted housing, as to familial status.","penalty":"A discriminating landlord may owe monetary damages and be ordered to rent the unit; under the LAD, fines and penalties up to $10,000 for a first offense (N.J.S.A. 10:5-14.1a(a)).","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant-paid credit report and application fees","requirement":"A landlord may charge a prospective tenant the cost of a credit or screening report and may request reasonable rental application fees; if a credit report leads to denial, the landlord must give the applicant the reporting agency's name, address and telephone number.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants screened by New Jersey landlords; the text states no cap on the report cost or application fees.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Prospective tenants"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"15 U.S.C.A. § 1681m","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":"The denial notice comes from the federal Fair Credit Reporting Act; the permission to charge for the report is stated by the New Jersey Department of Community Affairs.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
